<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Finding the Curve of Best Fit</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02291</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Finding the Curve of Best Fit</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4ca61545-a2f1-4b87-aeb9-c5601f1a0d3e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In a previous lesson, you learned how to find the line of best fit. A
        line of best fit is a line on a graph that best approximates the equation of the points in a
        data set or scatter plot.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">In this activity, we will learn how to find the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">curve of best fit</term>, which is the curve
        modeled by the equation that best approximates the points in a data set or scatter plot.
        Since our data set is based on a quadratic function, we must use a curve instead of a line.
        This process, called <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">quadratic regression</term>,
        is the process of determining the equation of a parabola that best fits a data set.</para>
        <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00028">Examine the data set and graph given in the Desmos graphing tool below.</para>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/8f4b6af53f41cd6f466b15fc6e0c2a1b4e0047df.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
    </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise id="exer-00005">
      <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00003">Examine the data. Do they appear to you to form a line or a curve?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">They appear to be the start of a curve.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
        <para id="para-00047">We will use graphing technology to find a curve of best fit. Click the pointer below
          the table into entry box #2 to enter a new function. The cursor should start blinking.
          Enter the equation:</para>
      <para id="para-00007"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:msub>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:msub>
            <m:mi>~</m:mi>
            <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            <m:msubsup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msubsup>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            <m:msub>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:msub>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mi>c</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item>Notice that when you type the 1 after <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, Desmos automatically places it
          as subscript.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00004">
        <item>Also notice that we are not using an equal sign. The symbol ~ means approximately and
          tells Desmos to approximate a line or curve of best fit. It should be in the top-left
          corner of your keyboard, and you might press it using the Shift button. If you are using
          the Desmos on-screen keyboard, it will be on the ABC keyboard.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00005">
        <item>After pressing the subscript 1 after the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and the first <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, use the ^ symbol to tell
          Desmos you would like to enter an exponent.</item>
      </list>
    </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise id="exer-00007">
      <problem id="prob-00010">
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
        <item>Once you have entered the curve of best fit, you can press Enter. The curve of best
          fit will appear. Your screen will look like this:</item>
      </list>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/9b8e2ab70f7e853e494c0405586a967820edbd3f.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00008">Look in the lower-left corner of the screen. We will use the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>a</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, and <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>c</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> values to construct the equation for
        our curve of best fit.</para>
        </problem></exercise>

      <exercise id="df117ed1-5d4f-435a-8348-c5ae806d81dd" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">What is the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> rounded to the nearest
            hundredth?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0.86</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_48824ada-f643-4d81-b691-760b82408396" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00014">What is the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> rounded to the nearest
            hundredth?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00015"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>8.09</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5a9d0b4a-3348-4e1f-9f9f-dd1a56334c82" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Enter the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">What is the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> rounded to the nearest
            hundredth?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>16.53</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
         
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Write an equation in the form <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> for the curve of best fit
            using the values you found.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00024"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0.86</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8.09</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16.53</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00025">This is the equation for your curve of best fit.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00026">You may notice the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>R</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> value. The
        closer this value is to 1, the better your curve of best fit matches the data set. The <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>R</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> value for this
        data set is 0.9972. Since this value is relatively close to 1, this curve of best fit is a
        reasonably accurate match to the data set.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <media alt="...">
          <iframe src="../../media/desmos/7cd7011b2804b14f3a79ee8526025b8edcdb8289.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00027"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_8_12_2_SC"/></para>
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Finding the Curve of Best Fit </title>
        <para id="para-00029">Let's find the curve of best fit for a given data set.</para>
        <para id="para-00030">Use the Desmos graphing tool to determine the equation.</para>
        <media alt="...">
          <iframe src="../../media/desmos/997e7ebb197e7ac78d6d6ac633b8270a2fdd98e5.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
        <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Click the + symbol in the top left and
          select a table.</para>
        <para id="para-00032">Enter the data set as shown. Press Enter.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:msub>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                        </m:msub>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:msub>
                          <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                          <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                        </m:msub>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              </thead><tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 99.7 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 130 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
                <entry> 149 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 4 </entry>
                <entry> 172 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
                <entry> 175 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 11 </entry>
                <entry> 140 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00033">The data set will populate the graph as shown here.</para>
        <para id="para-00034">
          <media alt="A Desmos graph displays five plotted green points at (1,100), (2,150), (4,175), (7,200), and (11,250) on a Cartesian coordinate grid, with a list of corresponding x and y values shown on the left.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_14_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00035"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - On the left side, click in box #2 for a new item. Enter the
          instruction line below to generate a curve of best fit.</para>
        <para id="para-00036"><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msub>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:msub>
              <m:mi>~</m:mi>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:msubsup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msubsup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:msub>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:msub>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <list id="list-00007">
          <item>Remember from the activity that Desmos will automatically place the 1 as subscript
            after an <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</item>
        </list>
        <list id="list-00008">
          <item>Don't forget to use the symbol ~ that means approximately.</item>
        </list>
        <list id="list-00009">
          <item>Use the ^ symbol to enter an exponent.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00037">As soon as you enter the instruction line, Desmos will generate the
          curve of best fit.</para>
        <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the coefficients generated to write
          the equation for the curve of best fit.</para>
        <para id="para-00039">
          <media alt="Output summary for a quadratic regressionr-squared is 0.9958. A, B, and C are negative 2.75569, 37.0352, and 65.6605, respectively.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_14_05_02.jpg" width="483"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00040">Round each coefficient to the nearest hundredth.</para>
        <para id="para-00041">The curve of best fit is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2.76</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>37.04</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>65.66</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Finding the Curve of Best Fit</title>
      <para id="para-00042">Find the
          equation of the curve of best fit. Round each coefficient to the nearest hundredth.</para>
      <media alt="...">
            <iframe src="../../media/desmos/1a3f9b804707e785efa4d3e4170660ba07e2b73d.html" width="500" height="400"/>
          </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
            <problem id="prob-00006">
              <para id="para-00043"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00006">
              <para id="para-00044">Here is how to find the curve of best fit:</para>
              <para id="para-00045">Solve for the curve of best fit by entering <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msub>
                      <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:msub>
                    <m:mi>~</m:mi>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:msubsup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msubsup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:msub>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:msub>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>
              <para id="para-00046">The curve of best fit is <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>6.18</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4.59</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>25.42</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>